PROJECT NAME: Assign 1 Part 2 Client/Server Application

This is a standalone C++ program that emulates the route-finding server. 
It reads the coor- dinates of the trip start and end points from the client 
and give back the route information to the client.

Containning files:
	client.py
	map pngs
	edmonton-roads-2.0.1.txt
	server.cpp
	dijkstra.cpp
	dijkstra.h
	all source code and header files related to the graph and weighted graph class.
	Makefile
	README

To run and compile the file:
	run:
		g++ -std=c++11 -c server/server.cpp
		g++ -std=c++11 -c server/digraph.cpp
		g++ -std=c++11 -c server/dijkstra.cpp
		g++ -std=c++11 -o server/server digraph.o dijkstra.o server.o
		./server/server

	clean:
		rm -f inpipe
		rm -f outpipe
		rm -f digraph.o dijkstra.o server.o server/server

Input & Output:
	---(FOR DEVELOPERS ONLY, OTHERWISE, USE THE CLIENT)---
	Communication via a Pair of Pipes
	When the user selects both start and end points of a trip,
	the plotter program writes the respective coor- dinates at 
	once to a named pipe, called inpipe, as shown below:
		53.530870 -113.532972
		53.530870 -113.514991
	The route-finding server will read the coordinates from this 
	pipe to compute the shortest path. Subse- quently, it will write 
	the waypoints along the shortest path to another pipe, called 
	outpipe, so that the client can read the coordinates of the waypoints 
	and display the route by drawing a line between every two waypoints:
		53.53087 -113.53297
		53.51620 -113.53297
		53.51620 -113.51499
		53.53087 -113.51499
		E
	The character 'E' in the last line signals the plotter that there are no 
	more waypoint coordinates to read from the pipe for this route. Note, no 
	trailing spaces are at the end of each line, and there is a single 
	space character between the latitude and longitude of every waypoint. Each 
	line above must end with the newline character ('\n').


Containning Core Algorithm:

	Dijkstra’s Algorithm:
		Compute least cost paths that start from a given vertex.
		Use a binary heap to efficiently retrieve an unexplored
		vertex that has the minimum distance from the start vertex
		at every iteration.

Note:
	The client sends the route request in two lines without adding the 'R' character. 
	Additionally, the server does not send the number of waypoints and does 
	not expect an acknowledgement after sending the coordinates of each waypoint.
	The server only sends the 'E' character after writing all waypoints for 
	a route request to outpipe. Note that in the conversion to 100,000-ths 
	of degrees, the last digit of coordinates will be chopped off as explained before.
	After the coordinates of start and end points are sent to the server, 
	the client goes to a state where it waits on receiving waypoints’ coordinates
	from the server. When the route is displayed completely, the user can continue 
	scrolling around on the map. If they select new start and end points, a new path 
	should be retrieved from the server. Therefore, the server must wait for the next 
	request from the client as soon as it answers a route request query. Finally, when 
	the user closes the plotter window, the client writes a single character, 'Q', to 
	the pipe. The server must clean up and quit, if it reads this character from the 
	pipe.